# FLOW SHOP F|p_ij=1;outtree|Lmax is NP-complete

Source:
P. Brucker and S. Knust, *Complexity results for scheduling problems*, classification tables
(class "Flow-shop problems without preemption", minimal NP-hard entry
`F | p_ij = 1; outtree | L_max`); P. Brucker and S. Knust, "Complexity results for
single-machine problems with positive finish-start time-lags", Computing 63 (1999), 299-316,
doi:10.1007/s006070050036.
-/
module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Defs
public import CSLibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.UnitFlowShopOutTreeMaxLatenessDefs

@[expose] public section

namespace MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.UnitFlowShopOutTreeMaxLateness

open MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.UnitFlowShopPrecedenceMakespan
  (Prec PrecWellFormed IsSchedule UnitJobs)

/-!
# FLOW SHOP F|p_ij=1;outtree|Lmax is NP-complete

The unit-time flow shop of `UnitFlowShopPrecedenceMakespanWanted` (`m ≥ 1` machines,
operations `O_j1, …, O_jm` of unit length processed in machine order, each machine processing
one operation at a time, and job precedence: if `i` precedes `j`, no operation of `j` starts
before every operation of `i` is completed), with an out-tree precedence relation (out-forest:
each job has at most one immediate predecessor), an integer due date `d_j` for each job `j`, and
an integer bound `K`. QUESTION: is there a schedule whose maximum lateness `max_j (C_j - d_j)` is
at most `K`, where `C_j` is the completion time of job `j` (of its last operation)?

The unit jobs (`UnitFlowShopPrecedenceMakespan.UnitJobs`), the schedule
(`UnitFlowShopPrecedenceMakespan.IsSchedule`) and the acyclic precedence arcs
(`UnitFlowShopPrecedenceMakespan.PrecWellFormed`) are imported. Each job is listed with its
processing times (all `1`) and its due date; jobs are `Fin n` for `n` listed jobs. The
precedence relation is the one generated by the arcs `(i, j)` (`Relation.TransGen`); `a` is an
immediate predecessor of `b` when `a` precedes `b` and no job lies between them. Integers are
pairs `(a, b)` of naturals standing for `a - b`, as in `ZeroOneILPWanted`. Numbers use the
binary encoding.

Brucker and Knust classify the problem as NP-hard (in the strong sense). Membership in NP is the
certificate argument: the `n · m` start times of a schedule (each below `d_j + K` for its job
`j`) are checked in polynomial time.

Encoding: the instance is decoded from a bitstring with the repository's
canonical `MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding`. Undecodable bitstrings, and
bitstrings decoding to malformed instances, are no-instances.

NP-completeness is stated in the same form as the proved results in
`CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Karp`: membership in
`MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP`, plus a polynomial-time many-one
reduction from every language in `NP`
(`Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed`).
-/

/-- **FLOW SHOP F|p_ij=1;outtree|Lmax is NP-complete.** Source: Brucker and Knust's
classification (strongly NP-hard), citing Brucker and Knust (1999); membership in NP is the
certificate argument in the module docstring. -/
public theorem_wanted unitFlowShopOutTreeMaxLateness_npComplete :
    unitFlowShopOutTreeMaxLatenessDec ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP ∧
      ∀ L : MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem,
        L ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP →
          Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed L unitFlowShopOutTreeMaxLatenessDec

end MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.UnitFlowShopOutTreeMaxLateness
